select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b10-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Which ozone-depleting substances release chlorine and which release bromine? How many ozone molecules can one chlorine atom destroy?","back":"CFCs, HCFCs, carbon tetrachloride and methyl chloroform release chlorine; halons and methyl bromide release bromine. One chlorine atom can destroy 10,000–100,000 ozone molecules before it is removed.","explain":"It is the free chlorine or bromine atom, not the intact ODS molecule, that destroys ozone. Strong UV in the stratosphere breaks the ODS apart after it diffuses up there.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p275","order":1477},
{"id":"p1-b10-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Describe the chain reaction by which a CFC destroys ozone.","back":"UV breaks a C–Cl bond in the CFC, freeing a Cl atom. Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂. A free O atom then breaks ClO, releasing Cl again, which repeats the cycle.","explain":"Because chlorine is regenerated, it acts as a catalyst, so a single CFC molecule can destroy up to about 100,000 ozone molecules. The thinning was first measured seasonally over the South Pole.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p275","order":1478},
{"id":"p1-b10-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name the five categories of effects of ozone layer depletion described in the guide book.","back":"Effects on human and animal health (eye disease, skin cancer, infections); on terrestrial plants; on aquatic ecosystems (phytoplankton, larvae); on bio-geo-chemical cycles; and on air quality.","explain":"More UV-B changes species composition in forests and grassland, harms the early life stages of fish and shrimp, and changes sources and sinks of greenhouse gases. It also raises photo-dissociation in the troposphere, increasing harmful oxidants such as hydrogen peroxide.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p276","order":1479},
{"id":"p1-b10-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What countermeasures to ozone depletion does the guide book list?","back":"International agreement (Montreal Protocol) to phase out ozone-depleting chemicals; tax on ozone-depleting substances; ozone-friendly substitutes HCFC and HFC (lower ozone depleting potential, shorter life); recycling of CFCs and halons.","explain":"Note that HCFCs and HFCs, though gentler on ozone, are still greenhouse gases, so they are now counted alongside other GHGs for global warming.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p277","order":1480},
{"id":"p1-b10-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What would the earth's average surface temperature be without the natural greenhouse effect, compared with today?","back":"About −18°C instead of the present 15°C.","explain":"Water vapour, CO₂, methane and nitrous oxide absorb the long-wave infrared radiation emitted by the warm earth and re-emit part of it downwards. Short-wave solar radiation passes through relatively unimpeded. This natural warming is what made life on earth possible.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p277","order":1481},
{"id":"p1-b10-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What is the 'enhanced' greenhouse effect, and why is water vapour not counted among the gases driving it?","back":"The extra warming from human increases in greenhouse gases (CO₂ from fossil fuels, plus deforestation), on top of the natural effect. Water vapour is a GHG, but its atmospheric amount is not changed directly by human activity.","explain":"The human-influenced gases named are CO₂, methane, nitrous oxide, CFCs and ozone. The book estimates the earth's average temperature has risen by 0.75°C since 1880 because of human GHG emissions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p278","order":1482},
{"id":"p1-b10-013","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"According to the book's 2014 figure, what was the atmospheric CO₂ concentration, and why is cement a major CO₂ source?","back":"397 ppm (Mauna Loa Observatory, November 2014). Cement emits CO₂ both from fuel combustion and from calcination of limestone.","explain":"CO₂ is the most important GHG because of its abundance, and it persists for over 100 years. Coal-based power plants are the major CO₂ source in many countries, followed by vehicles; deforestation both releases stored carbon and leaves fewer trees to absorb CO₂.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p279","order":1483},
{"id":"p1-b10-014","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"How much carbon do the oceans and terrestrial biomass hold compared with the atmosphere, and how much excess human CO₂ do they absorb?","back":"Oceans hold about 50 times more carbon than the atmosphere; terrestrial biomass (trees, grasses) about 3 times more. Together they absorb about half of the excess CO₂ from human activity.","explain":"These natural sinks complement man-made carbon sequestration, which captures CO₂ from large point sources and stores it in depleted oil/gas reservoirs, deep coal seams or saline reservoirs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p279","order":1484},
{"id":"p1-b10-015","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name the main natural source and four anthropogenic sources of methane.","back":"Natural: wetlands. Anthropogenic: leakage in coal mining, natural gas pipelines and petroleum wells; rice cultivation; livestock (ruminant) digestion and wastes; landfills of municipal solid waste; wood burning.","explain":"Methane forms by anaerobic decomposition of organic matter (without oxygen). The book names landfills, livestock and wetland rice cultivation as the leading anthropogenic sources.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p279","order":1485},
{"id":"p1-b10-016","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name four human sources of nitrous oxide (N₂O) and its approximate atmospheric lifetime.","back":"Nitrogen fertiliser and manure; biomass burning; fossil fuel combustion in power plants; chemical industry (e.g. nylon production); ploughing that releases N₂O from soil; car catalytic converters. Lifetime is about 120 years (Table 10.1: 114 years).","explain":"N₂O has a GWP of 300 in the book's table, so even small emissions matter. Its concentration rose from a baseline of 275 ppb to 326 ppb.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p279","order":1486},
{"id":"p1-b10-017","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What are the largest man-made sources of perfluorocarbons (PFCs), and what are the main uses of SF₆?","back":"PFCs: primary aluminium production and semiconductor manufacture. SF₆: electrical insulation and power transmission equipment, the magnesium industry, semiconductor manufacture, and as a tracer gas for leak detection.","explain":"PFCs last several thousand years in the atmosphere, so all emissions accumulate. The book calls SF₆ the most potent greenhouse gas.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p280","order":1487},
{"id":"p1-b10-018","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Why are HCFCs and HFCs, which replaced CFCs, still a climate concern, and how are HFC emissions best contained?","back":"They are less destructive to ozone but are still greenhouse gases, and their production could increase greatly. HFC emissions (leaks from refrigeration and AC, end-of-life disposal, aerosols) are best contained by recycling the refrigerants.","explain":"Their shorter lifetime and lower concentration mean less warming per unit emitted than CFCs. CFCs themselves stay in the atmosphere for about 100–200 years because they are so chemically unreactive.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p280","order":1488},
{"id":"p1-b10-019","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Define Global Warming Potential (GWP).","back":"The ratio of the warming (radiative forcing) from one unit mass of a greenhouse gas to that from one unit mass of CO₂ over a period of time, i.e. its warming potential per unit mass relative to CO₂ (GWP of CO₂ = 1).","explain":"GWP accounts for both how strongly a molecule absorbs infrared and how long it stays in the atmosphere. Some gases are 140 to 23,900 times more potent than CO₂ over 100 years, so even tiny amounts of SF₆ or HFC-23 matter.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p280","order":1489},
{"id":"p1-b10-020","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Give the GWPs in Table 10.1 for CO₂, methane, nitrous oxide, perfluoromethane (PFC) and SF₆.","back":"CO₂ 1; CH₄ 23; N₂O 300; perfluoromethane 5,700; SF₆ 22,000.","explain":"Among methane, N₂O, CFCs and PFCs, methane has the lowest GWP (a common exam trap). Lifetimes in the same table: CH₄ 12 years, N₂O 114, perfluoromethane 50,000, SF₆ 3,200.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p281","order":1490},
{"id":"p1-b10-021","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What share of global GHG emissions comes from CO₂ from fossil fuels and cement, and how large were global fuel-combustion CO₂ emissions in 2013?","back":"Almost 70% of global GHG emissions; fuel combustion emitted almost 36 Gt CO₂ in 2013.","explain":"Energy use is by far the largest source of GHGs. Agriculture (CH₄ and N₂O from livestock and rice) and non-energy industrial processes (fluorinated gases, N₂O) are smaller shares. For 2010, Figure 10.8 shows 91% of human CO₂ from fossil fuels and cement and 9% from land-use change; of it, about 50% stays in the atmosphere, 26% goes to land and 24% to the oceans.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p281","order":1491},
{"id":"p1-b10-022","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Per the guide book (2013 data), what was India's share of global emissions, its rank, total CO₂ and per-capita emissions?","back":"About 7% of global emissions; third largest CO₂ emitter after China and the USA; about 2.5 Gt CO₂ in 2013; per capita only about 1.6 t/year.","explain":"Coal-based power generation accounted for almost 70% of India's coal-related CO₂ emissions. These are the book's 2013 figures and have since changed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p282","order":1492},
{"id":"p1-b10-023","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"By how much did average sea level rise in the 20th century, and what was the largest contributor?","back":"About 20 cm. The largest contribution was thermal expansion of warming ocean water.","explain":"The book predicts a further 10–20 cm rise by 2030 and up to 1 m by 2100, with low-lying countries such as Bangladesh most at risk. Melting ice adds to the rise and also loses the reflective cooling of white ice caps.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p282","order":1493},
{"id":"p1-b10-024","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name six impacts of global warming and climate change described in the guide book.","back":"Any six: rising ocean temperature and sea level; snow and ice melting (glacier retreat); altered rainfall patterns; more extreme weather events; more severe heat waves; loss of biodiversity; spread of diseases (e.g. malaria); dwindling freshwater supply; food shortages.","explain":"Indian examples in the book: rising rainfall on the west coast and north Andhra Pradesh, falling monsoon rain in north-east India and parts of Gujarat and Kerala, and more extreme weather in West Bengal, Gujarat and Kerala.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p283","order":1494},
{"id":"p1-b10-025","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"When and where was the UNFCCC signed, when did it come into force, and what is its stated objective?","back":"Signed by over 160 countries at the UN Conference on Environment and Development (Earth Summit), Rio de Janeiro, June 1992; came into force in 1994. Objective: stabilise GHG concentrations at a level that would prevent dangerous anthropogenic (human) interference with the climate system.","explain":"Its short-term aim was for developed countries to return GHG emissions, especially CO₂, to 1990 levels by 2000. Long-term, stabilisation should come within a time frame that lets ecosystems adapt, keeps food production safe and lets economic development proceed sustainably. Developed countries were to take the lead.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p284","order":1495},
{"id":"p1-b10-026","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"When and by whom was the IPCC formed, and what did its 1995 report conclude?","back":"Formed in 1988 by the World Meteorological Organization and the United Nations. The 1995 report said the observed warming is 'unlikely to be entirely natural in origin' and that there is a 'discernible human influence on global climate'.","explain":"It also projected a 1.4–5.8°C rise in the 21st century (about 75% due to CO₂) and a further 0.1–0.9 m sea-level rise. The report changed the world's view of climate change and led to the 1997 Kyoto Protocol.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p285","order":1496},
{"id":"p1-b10-027","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Conference of Parties (COP), and which COP produced the Kyoto Protocol?","back":"The COP is the supreme body of the UNFCCC, where the Parties meet annually to promote and review implementation of the Convention. COP 3 produced the Kyoto Protocol in 1997.","explain":"Meetings held between COPs are called intersessional meetings. Kyoto set legally binding emission targets for developed (Annex I) countries for six GHGs, to be met over 2008–2012.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p286","order":1497},
{"id":"p1-b10-028","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What did the Kyoto Protocol commit industrialised countries to, and how does it differ from the Convention?","back":"Binding targets for 37 industrialised countries and the European Community to cut GHG emissions by an average of 5% below 1990 levels over 2008–2012. The Convention only encouraged them to stabilise emissions; the Protocol commits them to it.","explain":"It places a heavier burden on developed nations under 'common but differentiated responsibilities', because they caused most of today's GHG levels through more than 150 years of industry. It entered into force in 2005 after Russia ratified; the USA (about 25% of global GHGs) did not ratify.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p286","order":1498},
{"id":"p1-b10-029","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What is a Party's 'assigned amount' under Kyoto, and what was the EU 'bubble'?","back":"Assigned amount: the maximum emissions (in CO₂ equivalent) a Party may emit over the commitment period to meet its target. Bubble: the 15 EU states shared one overall target and redistributed it among themselves as different individual targets.","explain":"Table 10.2 targets (vs 1990): EU-15 and several others −8%, USA −7%, Canada, Hungary, Japan and Poland −6%, Croatia −5%, New Zealand, Russia and Ukraine 0%, Norway +1%, Australia +8%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p287","order":1499},
{"id":"p1-b10-030","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish Annex I, Annex II and Non-Annex I Parties under the UNFCCC.","back":"Annex I: industrialised OECD countries plus economies in transition (EIT, e.g. Russia, Baltic and Central/Eastern European states). Annex II: the OECD members of Annex I (not EITs), who must provide finance and technology transfer to developing countries. Non-Annex I: mostly developing countries.","explain":"Developing countries are recognised as especially vulnerable, for example those with low-lying coasts or prone to drought. Their needs are addressed through investment, insurance and technology transfer.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p288","order":1500},
{"id":"p1-b10-031","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name the three market-based mechanisms of the Kyoto Protocol.","back":"Emissions trading ('carbon trading'), the Clean Development Mechanism (CDM), and Joint Implementation (JI).","explain":"Countries must meet their targets mainly through national measures. The mechanisms are a supplement that lets them meet targets more economically and stimulates green investment. Emissions trading lets countries with spare emission units sell them to countries over their targets.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p288","order":1501},
{"id":"p1-b10-032","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"How do CDM and Joint Implementation differ? What credit does each earn?","back":"CDM: a country with a Kyoto commitment funds an emission-reduction project in a developing country (no commitment) and earns Certified Emission Reductions (CERs). JI: a project in another Annex I country (both have commitments) earns Emission Reduction Units (ERUs). Each credit = 1 tonne CO₂.","explain":"A JI project must give reductions or removals additional to what would otherwise happen; the host gains investment and technology. Most CDM projects have been renewable energy, followed by waste handling and disposal. CDM reductions from 2000–2007 could count towards 2008–2012 commitments.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p289","order":1502},
{"id":"p1-b10-033","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"List the stages of the CDM project cycle in order.","back":"1) Project preparation (identification, feasibility); 2) Project Idea Note (PIN); 3) Project Design Document (PDD); 4) host country approval by the DNA; 5) PDD made public for 30 days; 6) validation by a DOE; 7) validation report submitted; 8) registration by the CDM Executive Board; then implementation and monitoring, yearly verification, and certification and issuance of CERs.","explain":"The last three stages run throughout the project's life. Project preparation uses approved UNFCCC methodologies to show the project is real, measurable and additional.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p289","order":1503},
{"id":"p1-b10-034","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name the three types of small-scale CDM projects eligible for fast-track approval, with their limits.","back":"Type I: renewable energy projects up to 15 MW capacity. Type II: energy efficiency projects cutting energy use by up to 15 GWh/year (54 TJ) vs business-as-usual. Type III: other projects emitting less than 15 kt CO₂/year.","explain":"A fast track exists because the normal CDM process is too costly for small projects. Small projects also give big local sustainable-development benefits, small technologies (solar, wind, fuel cells) are promising, and CDM could lose public support if biased toward large capital-intensive projects.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p290","order":1504},
{"id":"p1-b10-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What is a Project Idea Note (PIN) in CDM, and what does it contain?","back":"An optional note of about five pages that informs the host country DNA and potential CER buyers. It covers project type and size; location; expected GHG reduction vs business-as-usual; suggested crediting lifetime; suggested CER price (US$/tCO₂e); financial structure; and other socio-economic or environmental benefits.","explain":"A PIN is not required, but it helps obtain early feedback on host-country sustainability requirements and buyer interest before the full CDM process. A feasibility study is also optional but helps prove additionality and shows ROI with and without CDM.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p291","order":1505},
{"id":"p1-b10-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What is the key mandatory document of the CDM cycle, and what crediting-period options does a project have?","back":"The Project Design Document (PDD). Crediting period: either one fixed period of 10 years, or up to three periods of 7 years each, with revalidation every seventh year.","explain":"The baseline methodology and baseline scenario in the PDD set how many CERs the project earns. The PDD goes to a DOE for validation and then to the CDM Executive Board for registration. Credits normally count from project start (from registration if the project began before it was registered).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p291","order":1506},
{"id":"p1-b10-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Who is India's National CDM Authority, and why is host-country approval required?","back":"The Ministry of Environment & Forests (MoE&F). Approval ensures governments keep sovereignty over their natural resources and confirms that the project meets the host country's sustainable development criteria.","explain":"The Designated National Authority (DNA) in the non-Annex I country evaluates and approves the project and is the point of contact. The completed PDD and reports are submitted to it.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p292","order":1507},
{"id":"p1-b10-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"How does validation differ from verification in a CDM project?","back":"Validation is done at the outset by a DOE, which checks the PDD against CDM requirements (after 30 days of public comment). Verification is the periodic (usually annual) ex-post check of the monitored emission reductions during operation, which form the basis for CERs.","explain":"Registration with the CDM Executive Board becomes final within a maximum of eight weeks unless a review is requested. After verification, the DOE issues a certification report, and CERs are issued unless a project participant or three Executive Board members request a review within 15 days.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p292","order":1508},
{"id":"p1-b10-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"In CDM, what is 'leakage', and for how long must a project be monitored?","back":"Leakage is an indirect increase or decrease in emissions outside the defined project boundary (e.g. changed consumer behaviour or reduced transport). Monitoring continues for the whole crediting period (3 × 7 years or 10 years).","explain":"At minimum, monitoring must cover project output, related GHG emissions, environmental impact and leakage, following the monitoring plan in the PDD. The monitoring report includes an estimate of the CERs generated.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p293","order":1509},
{"id":"p1-b10-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What levy applies to CERs from CDM projects, and what is it for?","back":"A 2% levy, used to help particularly vulnerable developing countries adapt to the adverse effects of climate change.","explain":"The book also notes that public funding for CDM must not divert official development assistance (ODA). Carbon funds come from international tenders, voluntary corporate initiatives, multilateral funds, EU purchase commitments and bilateral negotiations.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p293","order":1510},
{"id":"p1-b10-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What were the EU's '20-20-20' targets for 2020?","back":"A 20% cut in GHG emissions from 1990 levels; 20% of EU energy from renewables; and a 20% improvement in energy efficiency.","explain":"EU leaders set them in March 2007 and enacted them through the binding climate and energy package in 2009, aiming for a highly energy-efficient, low-carbon economy.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p294","order":1511},
{"id":"p1-b10-042","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"How does the EU Emissions Trading System (EU ETS) 'cap and trade' work?","back":"A cap is set on total emissions from covered installations and is reduced over time. Companies receive or buy allowances, which they can trade. Each year they must surrender enough allowances to cover their emissions or face heavy fines; spare allowances can be kept or sold.","explain":"The book says it covers over 11,000 power stations and industrial plants in 31 countries, plus airlines. Trading means emissions are cut where it is cheapest. Covered emissions were to be 21% below 2005 in 2020, with 43% proposed for 2030.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p294","order":1512},
{"id":"p1-b10-043","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"How did the Brundtland Commission (1987) define sustainable development?","back":"'Meeting the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.'","explain":"The report was 'Our Common Future'. A fuller definition is 'living on the Earth's income rather than eroding its capital', keeping use of renewable resources within their replenishment. Applied to energy, non-renewable inputs should be used only as far as they can be substituted in future, and renewable ones only at the rate they can be renewed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p295","order":1513},
{"id":"p1-b10-044","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"problem","front":"A 110 MW coal power plant's R&M raises efficiency from 28% to 32%. Specific coal consumption was 0.7 kg/kWh before. For 7000 h/year, same coal quality, find (a) coal saved per year and (b) CO₂ avoided at 1.53 kg CO₂/kg coal.","back":"New SCC = 0.7 × 28/32 = 0.6125 kg/kWh. Generation = 770 million kWh/yr. Coal saved ≈ 67,375 t/yr. CO₂ avoided ≈ 1,03,084 t/yr.","explain":"With the same coal quality, specific coal consumption is inversely proportional to efficiency. The saving per kWh times annual generation gives coal saved, and the emission factor converts that to CO₂ avoided. This is the book's short question S-1, worked here.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p297","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the specific coal consumption after R&M.","answer":"0.7 × (28/32) = 0.6125 kg/kWh.","explain":"Coal per kWh ∝ 1/efficiency when GCV is unchanged."},{"prompt":"Find annual generation.","answer":"110,000 kW × 7000 h = 77 × 10⁷ kWh (770 million kWh).","explain":"Capacity × operating hours."},{"prompt":"Find annual coal saved.","answer":"(0.7 − 0.6125) × 770 × 10⁶ = 0.0875 × 770 × 10⁶ = 67,375,000 kg ≈ 67,375 t/yr.","explain":"Saving per kWh × kWh per year."},{"prompt":"Find CO₂ avoided.","answer":"67,375 t × 1.53 = 1,03,083.75 ≈ 1,03,084 t CO₂/yr.","explain":"Apply the emission factor of 1.53 kg CO₂ per kg coal."}],"order":1514},
{"id":"p1-b11-001","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Why are renewable energy sources described as 'flows' while fossil and nuclear fuels are 'stocks'?","back":"Renewables come from essentially inexhaustible, continuously renewed sources (sun, wind, water, waves, geothermal heat, biomass), whereas fossil and nuclear fuels are finite stored quantities that get used up.","explain":"The book also notes that renewable energy can generally be used without releasing harmful pollutants and is also called non-conventional energy. Examples: wind, solar, geothermal, tidal, sustainably grown bio-energy and hydropower.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p301","order":1515},
{"id":"p1-b11-002","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is the solar constant, and what is its average value?","back":"The rate at which solar energy at all wavelengths is received per unit area at the top of the earth's atmosphere; on average about 1,368 W/m².","explain":"It varies by about 0.3% over the 11-year solar cycle, and each planet has its own solar constant. Don't confuse it with average insolation (342 W/m²), a common exam distractor.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p301","order":1516},
{"id":"p1-b11-003","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Define solar insolation. How does it relate to the solar constant and to the sun's angle?","back":"The solar energy striking a square metre of the earth's surface in a day. It is greatest when the surface is normal to the sun and falls with the cosine of the angle from the normal. The average incoming radiation is one-fourth of the solar constant, about 342 W/m².","explain":"Insolation is usually expressed in kWh/m²/day and sets collector size: a poor-insolation area needs a larger collector. The more slanted the sun's rays, the longer their path through the atmosphere and the more diffuse they become.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p301","order":1517},
{"id":"p1-b11-004","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is the 'solar window'?","back":"The period of the day, typically 9 AM to 3 PM, when maximum sunlight is available.","explain":"Solar collectors are sized and oriented to make best use of this window, when the sun is highest and its rays least slanted.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p301","order":1518}
]$cards$::jsonb);
